===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|secunde}}, {{m|enm|seconde}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|seconde}}, from {{der|en|ML.|secunda|}}, short for {{m|la|secunda pars minuta||second diminished part (of the hour)}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{sense|SI unit of time}} {{qualifier|abbreviations}} [[s]], [[sec]]; {{qualifier|symbols}} [[s]] {{qualifier|SI and non-scientific usage}}, [[sec]] {{qualifier|in non-scientific usage only}}
* {{sense|unit of angle}} {{qualifier|abbreviations}} [[arcsec]], [["]]

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|sĕʹkənd}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛk.(ə)nd/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛk.(ə)nd/|/ˈsɛk.(ə)nt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-second.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkənd|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|sec|ond}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Flashingsecond.gif|thumb|right|75px|A light flashing approximately once per second]]

# A unit of time historically and commonly defined as a sixtieth of a [[minute]] which the International System of Units more precisely defines as the duration of 9,192,631,770 periods of [[radiation]] corresponding to the transition between two hyperfine levels of [[caesium]]-133 in a ground state at a temperature of [[absolute zero]] and at rest.
# A unit of [[angle]] equal to one-sixtieth of a [[minute]] of arc or one part in 3600 of a [[degree]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A short, [[indeterminate]] amount of time.
#: {{ux|en|I'll be there in a '''second'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Aluminum Surfaces: a Guide to Alloys, Finishes, Fabrication and Maintenance in Architecture and Art|year=2020|author=L. William Zahner|authorlink=L. William Zahner|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9781119541752|oclc=1114972586|page=287|chapter=Corrosion Characteristics|passage=Exposure of aluminum to the air causes a near instantaneous oxide. So rapid is the oxidation that it is safe to say you never see aluminum that has no oxide on its surface... The initial exposure of aluminum, regardless of alloy, will form a thin oxide film on the surface the '''second''' it is exposed.}}

